e0e4bca2-1621-4ac8-8e4b-822174861eef,2026-08-15T06:56:18.994Z,30965180,12,"U1: hey Flexi what is the HCF of 448 and 1024 by continue division method
U2: the smallest prime number to divide 448 and 1024 both evenly
U3: 448 divided by 2
U4: the smallest prime number to divide 448 and 1024 both evenly 448 / 2 44 / 2 1024 / 2
U5: 1024 / 2
U6: smallest prime number which will divide 224 and 512
U7: smallest prime number which will Divide 112 and 256
U8: 56/2. 128/2
U9: 28 / 2 and 64 / 2
U10: smallest prime number which divides 7 and 16 evenly
U11: 2 to the power of 6
U12: HCF of 448 and 1024","Keep dividing by \(2\) since both numbers are even: \[ \gcd(448,1024)=\gcd(224,512)=\gcd(112,256)=\gcd(56,128)=\gcd(28,64)=\gcd(14,32)=\gcd(7,16) \] Now \(7\) is odd and doesn’t divide \(16\), so we stop. We divided by \(2\) a total of \(6\) times, so: \[ \text{HCF} = 2^6 = 64 \] **HCF of \(448\) an",Procedural,,,,,Academic,,,,1957,64,0.000885,Skipped full rating by Level-1 gate: gate_llm,1,skip,Academic,Homework / Assignment Help,gate_llm,gate_llm
