U11: What happens at other angles","**At other common angles, \(\sin\), \(\cos\), and \(\tan\) have standard exact values (the “special angles”).** Here are the ones used most in Class 11:

\[
\begin{array}{c|c|c|c}
\theta & \sin\theta & \cos\theta & \tan\theta\\ \hline
0^\circ & 0 & 1 & 0\\
30^\circ & \frac12 & \frac{\sqrt3}{2} & \frac{\sqrt3}{3}\\
45^\circ & \frac{\sqrt2}{2} & \frac{\sqrt2}{2} & 1\\
60^\circ & \frac{\sqrt3}{2} & \frac12 & \sqrt3\\
90^\circ & 1 & 0 & \text{not defined}
\end{array}
\]

**Key thing:** \(\tan\theta=\dfrac{\sin\theta}{\cos\theta}\). So at \(90^\circ\), \(\cos 90^\circ=0\), and dividing by 0 is why \(\tan 90^\circ\) is **not defined**.

Do you want the values for angles in other quadrants too (like \(120^\circ, 135^\circ, 150^\circ\))?",Procedural,5,5,5,10,Academic,,,1.0000,14046,229,0.003420,"The bot correctly answered all user queries with accurate mathematical explanations and no evident failures. The user did re-ask for clarifications, but the bot responded appropriately without ignoring corrections or losing context. There were no slotfills, image uploads, or board/class mismatches, resulting in a flawless session.",0,full_rate,Academic,Concept Explanation,explicit_frustration | gate_llm,gate_llm
