## **A) Reflection (परावर्तन) — Correct Exam Definitions (English) + Meaning (Hindi)**

**1) Reflection (Definition):** 
**Reflection is the phenomenon of light returning into the same medium after striking a surface.** 
**मतलब:** प्रकाश किसी सतह से टकराकर **उसी माध्यम** (जैसे हवा) में वापस लौटता है।

**2) Incident Ray:** 
**The ray of light that falls on a surface is called the incident ray.** 
**मतलब:** सतह पर गिरने वाली किरण।

**3) Reflected Ray:** 
**The ray of light that returns from the surface after reflection is called the reflected ray.** 
**मतलब:** टकराने के बाद वापस जाने वाली किरण।

**4) Normal:** 
**A line drawn perpendicular (at \(90^\circ\)) to the surface at the point of incidence is called the normal.** 
**मतलब:** जहाँ किरण गिरती है, वहाँ सतह पर 90° की रेखा।

**5) Angle of Incidence \((i)\):** 
**The angle between the incident ray and the normal is called the angle of incidence.**

**6) Angle of Reflection \((r)\):** 
**The angle between the reflected ray and the normal is called the angle of reflection.**

**7) Laws of Reflection:** 
(i) **The angle of incidence is equal to the angle of reflection** \((i=r)\). 
(ii) **The incident ray, the reflected ray, and the normal all lie in the same plane.** 
**मतलब:** तीनों एक ही समतल में होते हैं।

---

## **B) Refraction (अपवर्तन) — Correct Exam Definitions (English) + Meaning (Hindi)**

**1) Refraction (Definition):** 
**Refraction is the bending (change in direction) of light when it travels from one medium to another due to a change in its speed.** 
**मतलब:** माध्यम बदलते ही speed बदलती है, इसलिए प्रकाश मुड़ता है।

**2) Refractive Index \((n)\):** 
**The refractive index of a medium is the ratio of the speed of light in vacuum to its speed in that medium: \(n=\dfrac{c}{v}\).** 
**मतलब:** जितना medium light को slow करता है, \(n\) उतना बड़ा।

**3) Snell’s Law:** 
**For a given pair of media, \(\dfrac{\sin i}{\sin r}=\text{constant}\).** 
(यह constant वास्तव में \(n_2/n_1\) होता है, पर इतना लिखना अक्सर पर्याप्त रहता है।)

---

## **C) Mirror Terms (English‑medium, board style)**

**1) Pole \((P)\):** 
**The midpoint of a spherical mirror is called the pole.**

**2) Centre of Curvature \((C)\):** 
**The centre of the sphere of which the mirror is a part is called the centre of curvature.**

**3) Principal Axis:** 
**The straight line passing through the pole and the centre of curvature is called the principal axis.**

**4) Focus \((F)\):** 
**The point on the principal axis where rays parallel to it meet (concave) or appear to diverge from (convex) after reflection is called the principal focus.**

**5) Focal Length \((f)\):** 
**The distance between the pole and the principal focus is called the focal length.**

**6) Relation between \(R\) and \(f\):** 
**\(R=2f\)** (spherical mirrors के लिए)

---

