U6: 2marks and 5 marks and 7marks","## 2‑Mark Answers (Very Short)

1) **Who is Fufi?** 
Fufi is Trevor Noah’s childhood **pet dog**. He loves her and feels very attached to her.

2) **Why does Trevor feel betrayed?** 
He learns Fufi also goes to **another home/family** that feeds and cares for her. He expected her loyalty to be only for him.

3) **What is the main theme of the chapter?** 
The theme is **love vs. possessiveness**. It shows that love cannot be controlled like property.

4) **What lesson does Trevor learn?** 
He learns that **real love isn’t ownership**. Expectations can cause jealousy and pain.

5) **How is the chapter funny but meaningful?** 
It’s funny because Trevor reacts like a breakup over a dog. It’s meaningful because it teaches a deep lesson about love.

---

## 5‑Mark Answers (Short Paragraphs)

### 1) **Short Summary (5 marks)**
Trevor Noah loves his dog Fufi and believes she belongs only to him. One day he discovers that Fufi also visits another family who feeds her and treats her as their own pet. This shocks and hurts him, and he feels as if he has been replaced. Trevor’s mother helps him understand that love cannot be forced or owned. By the end, Trevor learns to see love with more maturity.

### 2) **Theme + Message (5 marks)**
The chapter mainly explores **love, attachment, and jealousy**. Trevor’s strong love for Fufi turns into pain when his expectations are broken. Through this incident, he learns that love is not something you can possess or control. The story shows that true love must allow freedom. It also teaches emotional growth and understanding.

---

## 7‑Mark Answers (Longer, but still clear)

### 1) **Lesson of the Chapter (7 marks)**
The chapter teaches that love is not the same as ownership. Trevor loves Fufi so much that he expects her loyalty to be only for him. When he finds out she has another family, he feels deeply hurt and jealous. His mother helps him understand that love can be shared and cannot be controlled. Fufi’s behaviour becomes a life lesson: expectations can lead to heartbreak when we try to “claim” someone’s love. Trevor learns to accept reality and to love in a more mature, unselfish way.

### 2) **Character Sketch of Fufi (7 marks)**
Fufi is a loving, friendly dog who easily wins people’s affection. She is playful and close to Trevor, which makes him believe she is completely loyal to him. At the same time, she is independent and follows comfort and care, moving between homes without guilt. Her “double life” shows that she is free-spirited rather than owned by one person. In the chapter, Fufi becomes a symbol of love that cannot be controlled. Through her actions, the story reveals a deeper truth about relationships and expectations.

### 3) **Character Sketch of Child Trevor (7 marks)**
Child Trevor is affectionate, sensitive, and deeply attached to Fufi. He treats her love as something special and exclusive, so he becomes possessive without realising it. When he discovers Fufi has another home, he feels shocked and emotionally broken, almost like a breakup. This shows his innocence and how strongly he depends on his feelings. He is also thoughtful because he learns from the experience instead of staying angry. By the end, he grows more mature in his understanding of love and freedom.

