U12: If tan theta is equals to minus 4 by 3 then sin theta is","\(\tan\theta=-\frac{4}{3}\) means the ratio \(\frac{\sin\theta}{\cos\theta}\) is negative, so \(\sin\theta\) and \(\cos\theta\) have opposite signs.

Use a reference triangle with legs \(4\) and \(3\). The hypotenuse is
\[
\sqrt{4^2+3^2}=\sqrt{16+9}=5.
\]
So \(|\sin\theta|=\frac{4}{5}\).

Because \(\tan\theta\) is negative, \(\sin\theta\) could be negative (Quadrant IV) or positive (Quadrant II). Therefore,
\[
\sin\theta=\pm \frac{4}{5}.
\]

