c. \( \frac{6 x+1}{2}=\frac{7 x-3}{3}-1 \)
U2: give the solution for b and c
U3: Give the solution of c also","\[
\frac{6x+1}{2}=\frac{7x-3}{3}-1
\]

Clear the denominators by multiplying **both sides by 6** (LCM of 2 and 3):

\[
6\cdot \frac{6x+1}{2}=6\left(\frac{7x-3}{3}-1\right)
\]

Simplify each part:

Left side:
\[
6\cdot \frac{6x+1}{2}=3(6x+1)=18x+3
\]

Right side:
\[
6\cdot \frac{7x-3}{3}=2(7x-3)=14x-6
\]
and
\[
6(-1)=-6
\]
So right side becomes:
\[
(14x-6)-6=14x-12
\]

Now solve:
\[
18x+3=14x-12
\]

Subtract \(14x\) from both sides:
\[
4x+3=-12
\]

Subtract 3:
\[
4x=-15
\]

Divide by 4:
\[
x=-\frac{15}{4}
\]

So **\(x=-\frac{15}{4}\)**.",Conceptual,5,5,3,8.666666666666668,Academic | Image Upload | Speech to Text,,,0.9500,12879,277,0.003228,"The bot correctly solved all three algebra problems and responded appropriately to user requests without any failures or misunderstandings. The session includes image upload and speech-to-text modalities, but no issues or errors were detected. User sentiment is neutral with no explicit positive or negative feedback.",0,full_rate,Academic,Homework / Assignment Help,same_intent_reask | gate_llm,gate_llm
