U3: Without performing long division, determine which of the following rational numbers will have terminating decimals and which will be repeating call and 7 by 20 4 by 15 and 13 by 250. Then check your answers by explicitly performing the long divisions and expressing these rational numbers as decimals.","**Rule (no long division):** In lowest terms, a fraction \(\dfrac{p}{q}\) has a **terminating** decimal iff the prime factors of \(q\) are only \(2\) and/or \(5\). Otherwise it’s **repeating**.

1) \(\dfrac{7}{20}\) 
\(20 = 2^2\cdot 5\) (only \(2,5\)) \(\Rightarrow\) **terminating**. 
Decimal: \(\dfrac{7}{20}=\dfrac{35}{100}=0.35\).

2) \(\dfrac{4}{15}\) 
Already in lowest terms. \(15 = 3\cdot 5\) (has a \(3\)) \(\Rightarrow\) **repeating**. 
Long division (or known value): \(\dfrac{4}{15}=0.2666\ldots = 0.2\overline{6}\).

3) \(\dfrac{13}{250}\) 
\(250 = 2\cdot 5^3\) (only \(2,5\)) \(\Rightarrow\) **terminating**. 
Decimal: \(\dfrac{13}{250}=\dfrac{52}{1000}=0.052\).

**Final answers:** 
- \(\mathbf{\tfrac{7}{20} = 0.35}\) terminating 
- \(\mathbf{\tfrac{4}{15} = 0.2\overline{6}}\) repeating 
- \(\mathbf{\tfrac{13}{250} = 0.052}\) terminating",Conceptual,5,5,3,8.666666666666668,Academic | Speech to Text,,,0.9500,12982,238,0.003212,"The conversation fully resolves the user's academic math questions with accurate and clear explanations. There are no technical or content failures, and no user corrections or complaints indicating dissatisfaction. The user sentiment is neutral as there is no explicit positive or negative feedback.",0,full_rate,Academic,Homework / Assignment Help,gate_llm,gate_llm
