U5: Solve agin","\(p\) AMs between 5 and 41 means the full AP is:
\[
5,\ A_1,\ A_2,\dots,\ A_p,\ 41
\]
So total terms \(=p+2\). Common difference:
\[
d=\frac{41-5}{p+1}=\frac{36}{p+1}.
\]

Now, \(A_3\) is the 3rd inserted mean, so it is the 4th term of the AP:
\[
A_3=5+3d.
\]
And \(A_{p-1}\) is the \((p-1)\)th inserted mean, so it is the \(p\)th term of the AP:
\[
A_{p-1}=5+(p-1)d.
\]

Given
\[
\frac{A_3}{A_{p-1}}=\frac{2}{5}
\Rightarrow
\frac{5+3d}{5+(p-1)d}=\frac{2}{5}.
\]

Substitute \(d=\frac{36}{p+1}\):
\[
\frac{5+\frac{108}{p+1}}{5+\frac{36(p-1)}{p+1}}=\frac{2}{5}.
\]

Write each numerator/denominator over \(p+1\):
\[
5+\frac{108}{p+1}=\frac{5(p+1)+108}{p+1}=\frac{5p+113}{p+1},
\]
\[
5+\frac{36(p-1)}{p+1}=\frac{5(p+1)+36(p-1)}{p+1}
=\frac{5p+5+36p-36}{p+1}
=\frac{41p-31}{p+1}.
\]

So
\[
\frac{5p+113}{41p-31}=\frac{2}{5}.
\]

Cross-multiply:
\[
5(5p+113)=2(41p-31)
\]
\[
25p+565=82p-62
\]
\[
565+62=82p-25p
\]
\[
627=57p
\]
\[
p=11.
\]

