### Checking (4)
You wrote: 
\(1450101.45010 \rightarrow 1450101.45010 \times 10^{5}\)

That is **not correct**, because multiplying by \(10^{5}\) makes the number **100,000 times bigger**.

To write \(1450101.45010\) in scientific notation, move the decimal so there is **one nonzero digit** on the left:

\(1450101.45010 = 1.45010145010 \times 10^{6}\)

So the correct scientific notation is:

\[
\mathbf{1.45010145010 \times 10^{6}}
\]

(And its **order of magnitude** is \(10^{6}\).)

