6c92c69b-19b0-4697-98b4-cc70d5279421,2026-08-15T11:14:52.957Z,1,3,"U1: Explain bbt
U2: Explain BPT
U3: Explain basic proportional theory","**Basic Proportionality Theorem (BPT)** says:

If a line is drawn **parallel to one side of a triangle**, and it cuts the other two sides, then it **divides those two sides in the same ratio**.

Example setup: In \(\triangle ABC\), let a line through points \(D\) on \(AB\) and \(E\) on \(AC\) be drawn such that \(DE \parallel BC\). 
Then BPT tells us:
\[
\frac{AD}{DB}=\frac{AE}{EC}
\]
So the “top pieces” on the two sides match in proportion to the “bottom pieces.”

A common related form (also true in this setup) is:
\[
\frac{AD}{AB}=\frac{AE}{AC}=\frac{DE}{BC}
\]
This is because the smaller triangle \(\triangle ADE\) is similar to the bigger triangle \(\triangle ABC\).

If you want, share a specific question (like values of \(AD, DB, AE, EC\)) and I can show how to use BPT to find the missing length.",Small Talk,5,5,3,8.666666666666668,Academic,,,0.9500,12458,235,0.003086,"The bot correctly and clearly explains the meanings of BBT and BPT and provides a detailed explanation of the Basic Proportionality Theorem without any errors or failures. There are no indications of user frustration or bot failures, so the session is rated highly for resolution and reliability with neutral user sentiment.",0,full_rate,Academic,Concept Explanation,same_intent_reask | paraphrase_reask | gate_llm,gate_llm
