Solve:
\[
\sqrt{3}x^{2}+7x+4\sqrt{3}=0
\]
Here \(a=\sqrt{3},\; b=7,\; c=4\sqrt{3}\).

**I’ll keep extra digits in the algebra and only simplify at the end.**

Use:
\[
x=\frac{-b\pm\sqrt{b^{2}-4ac}}{2a}
\]

Compute the discriminant:
\[
b^{2}-4ac=7^{2}-4(\sqrt{3})(4\sqrt{3})
\]
\[
=49-16(\sqrt{3}\cdot\sqrt{3})
\]
\[
=49-16(3)=49-48=1
\]

So:
\[
x=\frac{-7\pm\sqrt{1}}{2\sqrt{3}}=\frac{-7\pm 1}{2\sqrt{3}}
\]

Now split the two answers:

1) \(x=\dfrac{-7+1}{2\sqrt{3}}=\dfrac{-6}{2\sqrt{3}}=-\dfrac{3}{\sqrt{3}}=-\sqrt{3}\)

2) \(x=\dfrac{-7-1}{2\sqrt{3}}=\dfrac{-8}{2\sqrt{3}}=-\dfrac{4}{\sqrt{3}}=-\dfrac{4\sqrt{3}}{3}\)

**Final answers:**
\[
\boxed{x=-\sqrt{3}\quad \text{or}\quad x=-\frac{4}{\sqrt{3}}\; \left(=-\frac{4\sqrt{3}}{3}\right)}
\]

