U3: can you consult the table a standard electrode potentials and 3 substances that and oxidize peres ions under suitable conditions","**Yes—using the standard electrode potential table, any substance whose reduction potential is *more positive* than the \( \text{Fe}^{3+}/\text{Fe}^{2+} \) couple can oxidize \( \text{Fe}^{2+} \) to \( \text{Fe}^{3+} \) (under suitable conditions).** 
The relevant half-cell is:
\[
\text{Fe}^{3+} + e^- \rightarrow \text{Fe}^{2+} \qquad E^\circ = +0.77\text{ V}
\]
So pick oxidizing agents with \(E^\circ_{\text{red}} > +0.77\text{ V}\). Three common ones are:

- **\( \text{Cl}_2 \)** ( \( \text{Cl}_2 + 2e^- \rightarrow 2\text{Cl}^- \), \(E^\circ = +1.36\text{ V}\) ) 
- **\( \text{Br}_2 \)** ( \( \text{Br}_2 + 2e^- \rightarrow 2\text{Br}^- \), \(E^\circ = +1.07\text{ V}\) ) 
- **Acidified permanganate, \( \text{MnO}_4^- \) (in acidic medium)** ( \( \text{MnO}_4^- + 8\text{H}^+ + 5e^- \rightarrow \text{Mn}^{2+} + 4\text{H}_2\text{O} \), \(E^\circ = +1.51\text{ V}\) )

All of these have higher \(E^\circ\) than \(+0.77\text{ V}\), so they can **oxidize \( \text{Fe}^{2+} \rightarrow \text{Fe}^{3+} \)**.

