That means your method is working. Now you just need a **shorter + teacher-aligned** way so it doesn’t feel endless.

---

## What to do if the book is too long and your teacher doesn’t follow it

### 1) Make *teacher-based notes* your main source
Use these as your “syllabus” for tests:
- class notes
- what the teacher repeats
- what the teacher writes on the board
- worksheets / questions the teacher gives

**The long book becomes a backup**, not your main thing.

---

### 2) Convert each chapter into a 1–2 page “Exam Sheet”
For every sub-topic, write only:

- **Definition (1–2 lines)**
- **3–5 key points**
- **1 example (Indian example if possible)**
- **1 common question** your teacher asks

This makes the chapter short and easy to revise.

---

### 3) Stop trying to learn “all possible questions”
Instead learn **answer-building blocks**:
- Keywords/terms
- 4–6 main points
- one example/data line (if any)

Then you can handle many questions without memorizing each one.

---

### 4) Use the book only for 2 things (so it won’t feel long)
When you’re done with your short notes, open the book and do a quick check:

- **Add only new keywords** you didn’t have
- **Add only one extra example** if your notes are lacking

Ignore the rest.

---

## About sending pictures to AI
I can help explain a chapter **if you share a small portion** (like one sub-topic or one question). 
But don’t depend on copying full chapter images—your best scores will come from **your teacher’s pattern + your short notes**.

---

### What I need from you (choose one)
- Send **one photo of your class notes** from that Sociology chapter, **or**
- Type the **chapter name + 2 sub-topics** your teacher focused on

Then I’ll help you turn it into a short “Exam Sheet” format.",Conceptual,5,5,3,8.666666666666668,Academic | Not Math/Science | Speech to Text,,,0.9500,14686,297,0.003608,"The bot provides detailed, relevant, and context-aware academic study advice tailored to the user's schedule and subjects. There are no evident bot failures or misunderstandings, and the session maintains coherence despite the user's somewhat unclear speech-to-text input. User sentiment is neutral with no explicit positive or negative feedback.",0,full_rate,Academic,Study Plan / Syllabus,gate_llm,gate_llm
