**1) Coastal vs inland climate (why coastal is moderate)** 
**Land heats up and cools down faster than sea water.** Water changes temperature slowly, so places near the sea don’t get extremely hot or extremely cold. Farther inland, temperatures swing more.

**Example given:** 
- **Chennai (coastal):** about \(35^\circ\text{C}\) in summer and \(20^\circ\text{C}\) in winter → range \(\approx 15^\circ\text{C}\) 
- **Hyderabad (inland):** up to \(43^\circ\text{C}\) in summer and about \(12^\circ\text{C}\) in winter → range \(\approx 31^\circ\text{C}\)

**2) Sea breeze and land breeze (basic idea)** 
- **Day:** land becomes hotter than sea → warm air over land rises → cooler air from sea moves toward land = **sea breeze**. 
- **Night:** land cools faster than sea → air over sea is warmer and rises → cooler air from land moves toward sea = **land breeze**.

**3) Microclimate (what it means)** 
A **microclimate** is a small area where the climate is different from nearby areas (temperature, humidity, rainfall can change within a few metres to a few km).

**4) Urban Heat Island (city hotter than villages)** 
Cities have lots of **concrete/asphalt** and fewer trees. These surfaces absorb and re-radiate heat, so **cities become warmer** than surrounding rural areas, especially at night.

