The image shows a differential equation involving dy/dx and asks the student to find its order and degree.","**Given:**

\[
k\frac{dy}{dx}=\int \left[1+\left(\frac{dy}{dx}\right)^2\right]^{\frac23}\,dx
\]

Differentiate both sides with respect to \(x\):

\[
k\frac{d^2y}{dx^2}
=
\left[1+\left(\frac{dy}{dx}\right)^2\right]^{\frac23}
\]

The highest derivative is \(\dfrac{d^2y}{dx^2}\), so:

\[
\boxed{\text{Order}=2}
\]

Because the derivative has a fractional power, raise both sides to the third power:

\[
\left(k\frac{d^2y}{dx^2}\right)^3
=
\left[1+\left(\frac{dy}{dx}\right)^2\right]^2
\]

This is polynomial in the derivatives, and the highest-order derivative \(\dfrac{d^2y}{dx^2}\) has power \(3\). Therefore:

\[
\boxed{\text{Degree}=3}
\]

**Hence, order \(=2\) and degree \(=3\).**",Procedural,5,5,3,8.666666666666668,Academic | Image Upload | Speech to Text,,,0.9500,13420,315,0.002972,"The bot correctly answered the user's questions about order and degree of differential equations with no factual or procedural errors. The session included image uploads and speech-to-text inputs, which were handled appropriately. There were no bot failures or user complaints, so the session scores high on goal resolution and technical reliability, with neutral user sentiment due to repeated similar queries.",0,full_rate,Academic,Homework / Assignment Help,same_intent_reask | paraphrase_reask | near_duplicate_reask | same_question_number_reask | gate_llm,gate_llm
